## Decision and scope

Track the reports in [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) as one date-order incident, with separate corrective tasks for shared input handling and the booking-v3 hint. The student requested this incident boundary after the course repeatedly said our separate tickets described the same problem. It is a proposed grouping; course acceptance and support-desk adoption are still pending.

The source code explicitly classifies parser and hint as different bugs. We preserve that distinction as implementation tasks and require independent verification of each path. One incident does not imply one code change or a proven common internal defect. The incident page's claim that one queued fix resolves everything still requires validation.

These findings concern calendar invites in `sched-prod`, from the export through 4 October. They establish neither faults in the desk's other products nor current live queue counts.

## Incident coverage and corrective tasks

<IncidentReview />

For the shared input path, the tenant enters a day-first date and month-first interpretation swaps it. For booking-v3, the month-first hint prompts input that the form interprets in tenant day-first order. Both can turn an intended 7 April into 4 July; both contracts must be made consistent.

The desk noted five unusual descriptions, including one-day moves and an invite arriving before booking. Preserve them in the incident and investigate their original timestamps and invite data. Do not infer a separate root cause or prescribe a timezone fix from wording alone.

## Queue history

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports began in April; merging into the umbrella ticket began in July. The merge spike reflects accumulated reports being filed, not necessarily a new spike in failures. Merging continued afterwards. A known label or queued fix does not establish resolution.

## Proposed replacement for subject-only triage

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) remains unchanged as accepted history. This record proposes its replacement; the desk must adopt the rule before practice changes.

1. Read each body and establish the product, intended date, observed date, tenant locale, booking form and channel.
2. Check tenant settings. Browser locale and intake tags are clues, not reliable cause labels.
3. Use evidence to choose the incident boundary. Matching subjects alone are insufficient. Retain distinct implementation paths and fixes as explicit tasks within a shared incident when grouping them.
4. Preserve unusual symptoms and missing facts for investigation. Do not manufacture a new root cause from a different description.
5. Deduplicate exports by report ID. Each report counts once, even if its ticket or task assignment changes.
6. Review new reports, update counts and links, and verify every affected path before closing the incident. Re-read stale guidance against current evidence.

## What the existing explorer establishes

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer compares the source classifier's implementation causes. It shows that equal subjects can conceal different paths and different subjects can share a path. That is evidence for investigation, not an automatic decision about how many incident tickets to file. An unknown tenant requires more information.

## Handoff and verification

[Public incident #1](https://github.com/skarlark/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) contains all report IDs and both corrective tasks. Ticket #2 is closed as merged; it adds no reports. The earlier timing ticket was deleted with the student's approval after its evidence was preserved.

Validate tenant date order on app/email and booking-v2 paths. Validate the booking-v3 hint, validation and interpretation together on all three affected tenants. Check the generated invite's intended day and month in each case. For unusual timing reports, collect original invites, event and recipient timezones, stored timestamps and creation/delivery logs. Confirm deployment and reproduction results before declaring resolution; the export contains no such confirmation.

